In this lesson we'll look at how two shapes can be either the same shape at a different size, called similar, or exactly the same shape and size, called congruent. We'll start with similar triangles. Two triangles are similar when their matching angles are equal, and that means their matching sides all sit in the same ratio, called the scale factor. So every matching side of one triangle is the scale factor times the corresponding side of the other. Now, how do we actually build a similar shape? We use an enlargement. You pick a centre point and a scale factor. Every point gets pushed out along the same ray from that centre, and its distance from the centre is multiplied by the scale factor. A scale factor greater than one makes the shape bigger, between zero and one shrinks it, and a negative scale factor flips it to the other side of the centre and mirrors it. Next, congruence. Two shapes are congruent when one can be moved perfectly on top of the other using transformations. The three big ones are translation, which is a slide, reflection, which is a flip across a mirror line, and rotation, which is a turn about a fixed point. All three preserve lengths and angles, so the shape you end up with is identical to the one you started with. And here's a quick heads up: checking one matching side or one angle is not enough. Similarity and congruence are claims about every matching pair, so you need to verify them all. Now let's see it in action.